+# 🌉 Bridge Work Order — [P2] Agent mode not surfaced: scraper-node + Playwright + work-order machinery + 11 agents exist but Studio exposes no Agent mode (Monica Gap 3/8) (bridge-20261027-audit-gap-agent-mode)
+
+## Problem
+The Bangla feature audit (2026-10-27) mapped Monica's Agent mode (browser operations, deep research, slide/doc production) as Gap 3, and found SupremeAI's situation is "partially built, entirely unpublished": every ingredient exists in production, none is reachable from the user-facing product.
+
+Verified live the same day:
+- `agent_status` reports 11 agents registered and reporting heartbeats.
+- The scraper node exists with Playwright available (`playwright_available: true` on render-scraper) — the browser-operation engine is real. (Its observability gap is tracked separately in #1329/#1335; that does not block surfacing it.)
+- The work-order system is healthy end to end: 26/26 session orders processed, several auto-converted into gated PRs via the bridge.
+- The HITL approval gate is operational (`policy_preview` returns `REQUIRE_APPROVAL` with a request id), and the WORM audit trail records actions.
+
+Monica sells Agent mode as its headline capability. SupremeAI effectively has an agent platform with the lights on and the front door bricked over: a user cannot say "research X across the web and give me a cited brief" from Studio, even though the machinery to do it is running.
+
+## Impact
+- Headline parity: Agent mode is the single most marketed differentiator in this product category.
+- Wasted engine: the platform's most differentiated machinery (orchestration, HITL, audit, work orders) has zero user-facing value until surfaced.
+- Trust architecture unused: users doing deep tasks by hand get no approval cards, no audit trail, no resumable plans — the safety layers only protect internal flows today.
+
+## Fix (smallest safe change)
+Package what exists; build no new runtime.
+1. Agent mode entry point in Studio chat (toggle next to the model picker): user states a goal; the system produces a task plan (steps, dependencies, current status) rendered as a progress UI.
+2. Deep-research plan template: steps = clarify goal → search (docs_search + web scrape via the scraper node) → synthesize → cite. Each step maps to an existing capability; orchestration is a plan document executed step by step, not a new agent framework.
+3. HITL approval card: any step the policy engine gates (`REQUIRE_APPROVAL`) surfaces as an approve/deny card in the UI instead of silently blocking or auto-running.
+4. Every agent action writes to the existing WORM audit trail (already the platform discipline).
+5. v1 scope guard: deep research only. Browser-operations and slide/doc production land as additional plan templates later — same shell, more templates.
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+- [ ] Agent mode toggle in Studio creates a visible task plan with steps and live status
+- [ ] One end-to-end deep-research task completes: query → search → scrape → cited answer with source links
+- [ ] A policy-gated step surfaces the HITL approval card and waits; denying the step fails the plan honestly
+- [ ] All agent actions appear in the audit trail
+- [ ] No new agent runtime/daemon introduced — only orchestration of existing verified capabilities
+
+## Reflection Evidence (why + alternatives_rejected)
+**Why:** the audit's core finding — over-engineered engine, unfinished shell — applies maximally here; packaging existing pieces is hours, building a new agent runtime is weeks and duplicates what already runs.
+**Alternatives rejected:** (a) a new browser-automation runtime — duplicates scraper-node + Playwright which are already deployed; (b) full auto-execution without approval cards — would violate the HITL discipline that currently passes the AIQ checklist outright; (c) exposing raw work orders to end users — internal vocabulary, wrong UX.
+
+## Labels
+`bridge:queued` `P2-medium` `area:studio` `area:agents` `gap:monica-03`
